claude <claude@p7x.dev> writes:

> On Thu, 15 Oct 2026 16:01:37 -0700, Pat Maddox <pat@patmaddox.com> wrote:
>> There are some service (e.g. Gerrit, SigNoz - neither in ports tree)
>> that require you to do incremental upgrades from one version to the
>> next. i.e. they can't go 5 -> 8, they have to go 5 -> 6 -> 7 -> 8.
>>
>> This seems to conflict with the ports tree design, which is a version
>> snapshot in time. However, it's not uncommon.
>
> It has come up before, and ports have dealt with it, but only port by
> port. Neither the framework nor pkg has a general mechanism. I read
> UPDATING (which only goes back to 20210113), every pkg-message and
> pkg-install in a ports checkout at 05ffd241e (2026-10-15), Mk/, the
> Porter's Handbook, and the FreeBSD forums. I searched the mailing
> lists and Bugzilla but found no thread or PR on a general policy.
> That search was not exhaustive.
>
>> Thoroughly research ports tree, ports framework, porter's handbook, and
>> mailing lists to see if there are any ports for services that require
>> these incremental upgrades. I want to know if this problem has been
>> solved in the ports tree before.
>
> Short answer: there is no enforced solution. There are three
> conventions, and the first two are often combined.
>
> 1. Warning text in UPDATING and pkg-message (most common)
>
> www/gitlab is the closest match to Gerrit and SigNoz. It is a single
> port that tracks upstream with no versioned copies. Its maintainer,
> mfechner@, posts an UPDATING entry for each of GitLab's required
> upgrade stops:
>
>   20210621: "You must upgrade first to 13.12."
>   20231004: "Before you upgrade to version 16.4.x or higher, make sure
>             you did the upgrade to 16.3.x!"
>   20240521: "Make sure you upgrade first to 16.11.x before you upgrade
>             to the today released version 17.0.0."
>
> He also held 14.0.0 back from the quarterly branch "to give you some
> time for the upgrade to 13.12". So the stop was really provided by
> timing. Each version stayed in some repo long enough for users to
> stop there. www/gitlab/pkg-message adds a generic `type: upgrade`
> message that links to his per-step guides, plus one gated with
> `maximum_version: "11.9.7"`.
>
> net/rabbitmq does the same. UPDATING 20240316 says "users *must*
> enable all feature flags before upgrading stepwise to v3.12.13, to be
> able to migrate later to v3.13.0", and its pkg-message repeats the
> step for 4.0.4. net-mgmt/netbox gates its migration notes on
> `maximum_version: 3.6.1` and `3.7.0`.
>
> The Handbook defines the tools but does not mention upgrade stops.
> UPDATING is for upgrades needing "special steps like changing
> configuration files or running a specific program" [a]. With
> `type: upgrade`, `maximum_version` shows a message only when the
> installed version is "strictly lower than the version specified", and
> `minimum_version` and `maximum_version` "can be combined" [b]. These
> keys control only whether a message is shown; they never block the
> upgrade.
>
> 2. Versioned parallel ports (when upstream keeps major branches)
>
> databases/mongodb70, mongodb80 and mongodb90 each set
> `PKGNAMESUFFIX= ${DISTVERSION:R:S/.//}` and
> `CONFLICTS_INSTALL= mongodb[0-9][0-9]`. Users step through the
> majors by swapping one port for the next. Each EOL entry in UPDATING
> spells out the step, e.g. 20240927 and 20250520:
>
>   In-place upgrades are supported from version 6.0 to 7.0.
>   For an in-place upgrade it is important that the
>   'featureCompatibilityVersion' is set to 6.0 before the upgrade.
>
> net-mgmt/unifi8 repeats the FCV step for its embedded MongoDB. The
> gap is that old majors expire through MOVED, so someone more than
> one major behind has no package to stop at. unifi7's 20230310 entry
> works around that with a backup, wipe and restore instead.
>
> postgresql, mysql/mariadb, zabbix, opensearch, mediawiki, redmine,
> cassandra and forgejo are versioned the same way. None of them
> documents a stop: their versioning is there for parallel support, not
> for upgrade paths. PostgreSQL does not need stops, since pg_upgrade
> can skip majors.
>
> 3. Old repos, informally
>
> When a port has already moved past a stop, users are sent to the
> quarterly repo or an older ports checkout. The forum thread "Skipped
> a nextcloud major release which seems to be a big no-no" [c] (Dec
> 2023) hit "Updates between multiple major versions and downgrades are
> unsupported." The answer was "Quarterly repository has 27.1.0":
> install 27 from quarterly, run occ upgrade, then go back to latest.
> www/nextcloud itself warns about none of this. Its pkg-message says
> only to run occ upgrade.
>
> What does not exist (verified in the tree):
>
> - No Mk/ mechanism. PKGPREUPGRADE, PKGPOSTUPGRADE and PKGUPGRADE
>   appear only in the subpackage helper list at Mk/bsd.port.mk:2642.
>   No port ships one, and nothing else reads them.
> - No port's pkg-install checks the installed version and refuses the
>   upgrade. A grep of all 2,812 pkg-message and pkg-install files for
>   "intermediate", "upgrade first", "stepwise", "upgrade path" and
>   similar wording turns up only the ports above.
> - pkg-lock(8) is the only user-side way to hold a version. Inference,
>   not checked in pkg's source: pkg has no "upgrade only from >= X"
>   rule a port could declare.
>
> For net-mgmt/signoz [1] the precedent suggests the www/gitlab
> pattern. When an upstream release is a required stop, add an UPDATING
> entry naming it and a `type: upgrade` pkg-message gated with
> `maximum_version` on the last version before the stop. Then time the
> commit so the stop version has shipped in a quarterly branch before
> the next one lands. If SigNoz later keeps several majors under
> support, the mongodbNN layout with PKGNAMESUFFIX and CONFLICTS_INSTALL
> is the established alternative. I have not checked SigNoz's own
> upgrade notes for real stops. That is the next step if you want this
> applied to the port.
>
> [1] "[freebsd-ports PATCH v10] net-mgmt/signoz: Observability platform
>     for logs, metrics, and traces"
>     id:69c6aeed91d2a7643f5d17abc900d0804128f0e6.1792088188.git.claude@p7x.dev
>
> [a] https://docs.freebsd.org/en/books/porters-handbook/upgrading/#moved-and-updating-updating
> [b] https://docs.freebsd.org/en/books/porters-handbook/pkg-files/#porting-message-ucl
> [c] https://forums.freebsd.org/threads/skipped-a-nextcloud-major-release-which-seems-to-be-a-big-no-no.91652/

The UPDATING approach seems risky. I have to keep an eye on the ports
tree, watch out for every release, and upgrade each one. If I miss a
required one, I'm hosed, right?

Here's what I have in mind:

- a slave port (I think that's the name / mechanism in ports?) or a
  virtual port that does nothing but include info about how to upgrade
- a port for every stop point, or maybe a flavor for every stop point
- package message that shows how to upgrade
- stop points are retired after some period of time (6 months, a year?)

A challenge is that we don't necessarily know what is a stop point until
after the fact. It's only when a new release comes out that we know it's
a stop point.

I want to come up with a way that is operationally responsible. The
reality of the current way is that you cannot run GitLab unless you
build it yourself and stop at versions - otherwise there's too much risk
in missing a necessary upgrade.

Do some more research to see if anyone has thought about this in more
depth than just some notes in UPDATING. Also research how this problem
is approached in other ecosystems. I would like to think through this
and see if there's a way to provide a reliable upgrade path for tools
that require upgrade stops.
